<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latin America</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> America is the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> where Romance languages, chiefly Spanish and Portuguese, predominate. It was shaped by the colonial rule of the Spanish and Portuguese empires, which transformed its religion, languages and societies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> was one of the most stable states in nineteenth-century Latin America.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latin-america.jpg" alt="Latin America" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heraldry · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latin_America_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Argentina became the most prosperous and urbanised country in <b>Latin America</b> in the early twentieth century and, by some estimates, among the ten richest nations in the world by income per head before the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…interest in seeing the monarchy outlive him and made no effort to defend it, died in exile in Paris in 1891 having presided over one of the most stable and constitutional governments of nineteenth-century <b>Latin America</b> — a ruler whose personal virtues outlasted his political will to govern.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Unlike Spanish America, which broke into many republics, Brazil stayed one unit, united by the Portuguese language and Roman Catholicism, and became the largest country in <b>Latin America</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States recognised the republic in 1822, among the first <b>Latin American</b> states it recognised, and Britain concluded a commercial treaty with it in 1825.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…José Vasconcelos, minister of education from 1921, sponsored the murals of Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco and David Alfaro Siqueiros, which influenced public art in the United States and <b>Latin America</b>, and promoted a celebration of the country’s Indigenous and mestizo heritage that became central to national identity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>José Enrique Rodó's Ariel (1900), Horacio Quiroga, Juan Carlos Onetti, Mario Benedetti and Eduardo Galeano's Open Veins of <b>Latin America</b> gave it a literature read across the continent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
